[Music]  hey martin i just got back from a hike  can we go back to anna's problem with  authenticating her api  there's nothing like a hike to clear  your head that's for sure  in a previous episode we followed anna  as she researched and settled on using  json web tokens to secure  take a hike's rest api in this episode  we'll see the coach he wrote  anna is a developer working for take a  hike  a fitness tracking startup she built a  rest api to enable their users  to record hikes from mobile and web apps  she secured the api with json web tokens  in a previous episode we showed the code  for the rest api  it runs on cloud run so it's serverless  and doesn't require any server  maintenance  check out that episode if you want to  see how to write a rest api  here's what anna's prototype looks like  from this webpage  users should be able to sign in using  their identity provider of choice  once they have signed in they should see  their recorded hikes  users should only be able to see their  own hikes  anna reads up on firebase auth which  supports multiple identity providers  it also includes a default user  interface called firebase ui  the code for ana's prototype is linked  in the description below  let's look at the most important pieces  in her javascript code  anna initializes firebase ui which will  display one button  for each identity provider shown to the  user she also tells the firebase ui  library  which page to redirect to after the user  is signed in successfully  finally she renders the firebase ui  component  on the next page the one that users are  redirected to  when they log in successfully anna calls  the get  id token the string she gets back is a  signed  json web token which contains the user's  unique  id after fetching the id token anna's  code requests the user's hikes  by calling the server side rest api her  code includes the id token  in the authorization header of the call  as we saw last time  anna implemented the server-side rest  